Delivered as `pipeline_refactored.py`. Here's what changed and why:

**Before → After**

| Original | Refactored |
|---|---|
| Hardcoded `DB_PATH`, `LOG_FILE`, `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASS` | All config reads from environment variables (`os.environ.get()`), with sensible fallbacks |
| `"INSERT INTO errors VALUES ('%s', '%s', %d)" % (...)` — SQL injection via string interpolation | Parameterized queries with `?` placeholders (`INSERT INTO errors (dt, message, count) VALUES (?, ?, ?)`) |
| One monolith `proc_data()` doing parsing + aggregation + DB + HTML | Four-phase ETL: `extract_logs()` → `transform_data()` → `load_to_db()` + `generate_html()`, each with helper sub-functions |
| `line.split(" ")` with fragile index magic (`s[2]`, `s[3]`, etc.) and manual string concatenation for error messages | Three compiled regexes: `_LOG_LINE_RE` (full line), `_USER_EVENT_RE`, `_API_CALL_RE` — named groups, proper validation, silent skip on malformed lines |
| No type hints, no docstrings | Full type hints + docstrings on every public function; detailed return type info on the extract phase |
| `HTML` output concatenated with `+` — no escaping | `html.escape()` on user-content fields (error messages, endpoint names) |

**Verified:** running the refactored script with the sample log data produces an identical `report.html` (same sections, same values, same structure) and populates the same DB tables. The original `pipeline.py` is left in place untouched; only the new file was added.
